  "query_preview": "..\n\nAnother pleasant day is in store for SE Mi as high pressure \nmaintains primary influence while drifting into upstate NY by this \nevening. Temperatures respond to the core of cooler and less humid \nair exiting eastward along with the surface high. Full sun and wind \nreturning SW over the region are able to lift readings into the mid \n80s, especially in the Tri Cities area. The warmer temperatures \noccur with typical mid summer humidity as surface Td creeps up to \naround 60 F most areas by this evening. \n\nAfter dry and mild conditions tonight, the forecast focus turns to \nthe broad central Canadian low pressure system that is pulling a \ncold front slowly into the northern/western Great Lakes during \nWednesday. Emphasis is on slowly as model projections show it taking \nabout 36 hours to move from the west end of Lake Superior this \nmorning into central Lower Mi by Wednesday evening. The leading \nmoisture axis has also been mostly inactive at night due to weak low \nlevel jet forcing as the system approached from the Plains Sunday \nnight and into the upper Midwest early this morning. That could \nchange tonight into Wednesday morning as upper level jet entrance \nregion forcing becomes more favorable for larger scale vertical \nmotion and to support a stronger low to mid level wind field. Longer \nlasting showers/storms from surface based convection this afternoon \nthen have a chance to combine with a nocturnal component tonight and \ngraze the Saginaw Valley by Wednesday afternoon. \n\nThe front takes the remainder of Wednesday afternoon and night to \nmake progress into southern Lower Mi. It does so while a component \nof surface based convection develops along and near the boundary as \nthe broad mid level trough and entrance region of the upper jet \nmaintain support for larger scale ascent. Consensus of 00Z regional \nand global deterministic models show a generous QPF response to the \nfavorable larger scale environment focused within the low level \nfrontal zone that takes all of Thursday and much of Thursday night \nto reach the Ohio border. The weak link in the severe storm \nparameter space is mainly lower end CAPE values in an otherwise \nrespectable SW unidirectional flow. This is in line with the Day 3 \ngeneral thunder outlook with an eye toward a marginal risk for \nexcessive rainfall. \n\nThe extended range model output gives the front a chance to settle \nsouth of the Ohio border by Friday morning where it dissipates \nduring the day. It leaves a tenuous scenario in place for dry \nweather Friday and Saturday while the large closed low remains \nanchored over far northern Ontario. The system has a chance to \nproduce another cold front in the Great Lakes by Sunday.",
